### Description

The [docs for this schematic](https://angular.dev/guide/testing/migrating-to-vitest#overview) say that it

> Converts fail() to Vitest's vi.fail().

but it actually replaces `fail('xyz')` with `throw New Error('xyz')`.  Sometimes the original `fail` call would have run in a context where an exception would be swallowed by the caller, so this produces the wrong behavior in cases like

```
myObservable.subscribe({
  next: () => { fail('should have been an error'); },
  error: err => { expect(err).toBeInstanceOf(FooError); }
})
```

As an aside, I'm new to vitest, but it looks like there is not actually a `vi.fail()` method?  I do see `assert.fail()` so that's what I'm planning to use for now.

### Minimal Reproduction

Create a spec file containing

```
// Invokes callback, passing 2
function twoCaller(cb: (x: number) => void) {
  try {
    cb(1);
  } catch (err) {
    console.log('oops', err);
  }
}

describe('twoCaller', () => {
  it('calls with two', () => {
    const failOnOne = (x: number) => {
      if (x !== 2) {
        fail(`oh no! ${x}`);
      }
    };

    twoCaller(failOnOne);
  });
});
```

then run `ng g @schematics/angular:refactor-jasmine-vitest`.  Note that the original test should fail (because the callback was invoked with the wrong value) but after the schematic runs it will erroneously pass (because the thrown exception is swallowed in the catch-block).

